@page
@model UISampleSpark.UI.Pages.EmployeeRazor.IndexModel

@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Razor Pages employee directory — page models and handlers";
    ViewData["Description"] = "The UISampleSpark employee directory as Razor Pages: each page owns its markup and handlers, with no separate controller.";
    ViewData["Keywords"] = "ASP.NET Core, Razor Pages, CRUD operations, employee management, Razor Pages tutorial, MVC vs Razor Pages, ASP.NET Core tutorial";
}

<div class="container mt-4">
    <div class="card shadow-sm">
        <div class="card-body">
            <h1 class="h2 card-title text-center">Razor Pages employee directory</h1>
            <p class="text-muted text-center">Pattern 03 · Each page owns its markup and its handlers. OnGet loads, OnPost saves.</p>
            <p class="text-center">
                <a class="btn btn-primary" asp-page="Create">Add employee</a>
            </p>
            <table id="EmployeeTable" class="table table-bordered table-hover table-striped">
                <thead class="table-dark">
                    <tr>
                        <th>@Html.DisplayNameFor(model => model.Employee[0].Name)</th>
                        <th>@Html.DisplayNameFor(model => model.Employee[0].Gender)</th>
                        <th>@Html.DisplayNameFor(model => model.Employee[0].Age)</th>
                        <th>@Html.DisplayNameFor(model => model.Employee[0].State)</th>
                        <th>@Html.DisplayNameFor(model => model.Employee[0].Country)</th>
                        <th>@Html.DisplayNameFor(model => model.Employee[0].Department!.Name)</th>
                        <th>Actions</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var item in Model.Employee)
                    {
                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <img src="~/images/@item.ProfilePicture" class="rounded-circle" height="40" width="40" asp-append-version="true" />
                                <a asp-page="./Edit" asp-route-id="@item.Id">@item.Name</a>
                            </td>
                            <td>@Html.DisplayFor(modelItem => item.Gender)</td>
                            <td>@Html.DisplayFor(modelItem => item.Age)</td>
                            <td>@Html.DisplayFor(modelItem => item.State)</td>
                            <td>@Html.DisplayFor(modelItem => item.Country)</td>
                            <td>@Html.DisplayFor(modelItem => item.Department!.Name)</td>
                            <td>
                                <a asp-page="./Edit" asp-route-id="@item.Id" title="Edit Employee" aria-label="Edit employee">
                                    <i class="icon icon-pencil" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                </a>
                                <a asp-page="./Details" asp-route-id="@item.Id" title="View Details">
                                    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" fill="currentColor" class="icon icon-eye" viewBox="0 0 16 16">
                                        <path d="M16 8s-3-5.5-8-5.5S0 8 0 8s3 5.5 8 5.5S16 8 16 8zM1.173 8a13.133 13.133 0 0 1 1.66-2.043C4.12 4.668 5.88 3.5 8 3.5c2.12 0 3.879 1.168 5.168 2.457A13.133 13.133 0 0 1 14.828 8c-.058.087-.122.183-.195.288-.335.48-.83 1.12-1.465 1.755C11.879 11.332 10.119 12.5 8 12.5c-2.12 0-3.879-1.168-5.168-2.457A13.134 13.134 0 0 1 1.172 8z"></path>
                                        <path d="M8 5.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 0 0 5 2.5 2.5 0 0 0 0-5zM4.5 8a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 7 0 3.5 3.5 0 0 1-7 0z"></path>
                                    </svg>
                                </a>
                                <a asp-page="./Delete" asp-route-id="@item.Id" title="Delete Employee" aria-label="Delete employee">
                                    <i class="icon icon-trash-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                </a>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="container mt-4">
    <div class="card shadow-sm">
        <div class="card-body">
            <h2 class="h3 card-title">How this pattern works</h2>
            <p class="card-text">
                A Razor Page keeps its markup and the code that serves it side by side. There's no separate controller: the page model's handlers load data and process forms. For page-by-page CRUD, that's less ceremony than MVC.
            </p>
            <h3 class="h4 card-title">Where it came from</h3>
            <p class="card-text">
                Razor Pages arrived in ASP.NET Core 2.0 to cut the number of files a simple page needs. It grew out of the older ASP.NET Web Pages framework.
            </p>
            <h3 class="h4 card-title">Good habits</h3>
            <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
                <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Use view models:</strong> keep database entities out of your pages.</li>
                <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Keep page models thin:</strong> business logic belongs in services, not handlers.</li>
                <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Use tag helpers:</strong> they keep Razor markup readable.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 class="h4 card-title">Trade-offs</h3>
            <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
                <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Good for:</strong> page-centric CRUD, a quick start, and UI and logic in one place.</li>
                <li class="list-group-item"><strong>Watch for:</strong> less control over routing and structure once an app outgrows simple pages.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@section Scripts {
    <script type="text/javascript">
        $(document).ready(function () {
            $('#EmployeeTable').DataTable();
        });
    </script>
}
